---
# Its own Deployment rather than a container in prowlarr's pod, deliberately: a sidecar shares the
# pod's network namespace, and "reachable only on localhost by its own pod" would be tighter than
# Compose but would also mean prowlarr and a headless browser rendering hostile pages share a
# netns and a lifecycle. Kept separate, with the NetworkPolicy beside it doing the isolation.
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: flaresolverr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  replicas: 1
  # No authoritative state: /config is a browser profile cache the app rebuilds on first use
  # (see the note in tasks/main.yml), so it moved to an emptyDir and the RWO constraint that
  # forced Recreate is gone. maxUnavailable 0 and maxSurge 1 are what SHOULD make this
  # zero-downtime — on a single replica maxUnavailable 0 forces the new pod Ready before the
  # old one is removed, where the 25% default would round down to 0 anyway but says nothing
  # about intent. This is unverified until the slice-1 measurement (measure_rollout_gap.py)
  # actually runs against a live deploy — no deploy has been authorised on this branch, so
  # "zero-downtime" here is intent, not a measured result.
  #
  # Conversion-gate note: only the write-model half of the gate was confirmed against
  # FlareSolverr's behaviour (the /config cache claim above, from this role's own prior
  # investigation). The multi-instance half was NOT confirmed — maxSurge: 1 means two
  # flaresolverr pods can run behind one Service simultaneously for the duration of a rollout,
  # and nothing here confirms from FlareSolverr's own docs that this is safe. Assessed risk is
  # low: Prowlarr drives FlareSolverr with sessionless `request.get`/`request.post` calls (a
  # throwaway browser per request), not `sessions.create`, so there is no per-pod session state
  # for a second pod to diverge from or lose. That is an inference from how Prowlarr's indexer
  # proxy integration is used here, not a documented confirmation from FlareSolverr's own API
  # docs — recorded honestly as a waiver, not papered over.
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1
      maxUnavailable: 0
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: flaresolverr
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: flaresolverr
    spec:
      enableServiceLinks: false
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
      priorityClassName: homelab-standard
      # Preference, never a pin: daniel-box carries the storage- and VIP-pinned workloads and runs the hotter of the two nodes, so latency-insensitive services default here.
      # Weighted so the pod still schedules if daniel-server is cordoned or full.
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            - weight: 100
              preference:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values:
                      - daniel-server
      containers:
        - name: flaresolverr
          image: {{ prowlarr_k8s_flaresolverr_image }}
          ports:
            - containerPort: {{ prowlarr_k8s_flaresolverr_port }}
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              # cap_drop: ALL with nothing added back, matching the compose template. Unlike the
              # LSIO images this one does not chown a config tree on startup, so it needs none of
              # the s6 privilege-drop set.
              drop:
                - ALL
          env:
            - name: LOG_LEVEL
              value: info
            - name: TZ
              value: "{{ tz }}"
          volumeMounts:
            - name: config
              mountPath: /config
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: {{ prowlarr_k8s_flaresolverr_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 10
            periodSeconds: 10
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: {{ prowlarr_k8s_flaresolverr_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 60
            periodSeconds: 30
          resources:
            limits:
              cpu: "{{ prowlarr_k8s_fs_cpu_limit }}"
              memory: {{ prowlarr_k8s_fs_mem_limit }}
            requests:
              cpu: "{{ prowlarr_k8s_fs_cpu_request }}"
              memory: {{ prowlarr_k8s_fs_mem_request }}
      volumes:
        - name: config
          # sizeLimit preserves the cap the retired 1Gi PVC used to provide. Most siblings here
          # use a bare `emptyDir: {}`, but this one holds a Chrome profile: uncapped, runaway
          # growth becomes node ephemeral-storage pressure and the kubelet evicts OTHER pods on
          # the node, turning one browser's disk leak into someone else's outage. Capped, the
          # blast radius is the pod that caused it.
          emptyDir:
            sizeLimit: 1Gi
